argon-utils (0.1.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * argond keeps a record of the UPS clock's drift that survives reboots,
    in /var/lib/argon-utils/clock.log -- the journal on Raspberry Pi OS
    does not -- and argonctl rtc reports the drift rate once there is a
    day of uncorrected history. Removed on purge.

 -- Simon Keimer <dc0sk@mailbox.org>  Fri, 18 Sep 2026 18:30:00 +0200

argon-utils (0.1.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Scheduled wake: `argonctl poweroff --wake-at TIME` has argond set a UPS
    wake, read it back, and only then power off a minute later. Needs root
    and mode "full".
  * argond parks any wake schedule that would come due, or has passed, on a
    running machine -- a wake very likely works by cutting and restoring
    power, which on a running machine would be an abrupt power cut.
  * argond listens on /run/argon-utils/control.sock (root only) for these
    requests: the first piece of its control interface.
  * argonctl zigbee: identify the Zigbee module; --probe reads its firmware
    version without disturbing it.
  * argonctl rtc: read the UPS clock and wake schedule.

 -- Simon Keimer <dc0sk@mailbox.org>  Fri, 18 Sep 2026 17:30:00 +0200

argon-utils (0.1.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Manual pages: argond(8), argonctl(1), argon-tray(1), generated from the
    same definitions as --help.
  * The programs now report the package version: --version said 0.1.0
    through 0.1.3.
  * argond is described as what it is -- a UPS, OLED and fan daemon. It
    was described as handling the case button, which it does not.

 -- Simon Keimer <dc0sk@mailbox.org>  Fri, 18 Sep 2026 15:00:00 +0200

argon-utils (0.1.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * argond keeps the UPS clock right: it compares it with the system clock
    at startup and every six hours, and sets it when more than 2 s off --
    only in full mode, and only while the system clock is NTP-synchronised.
    Every check logs the offset it found. Turn it off
    with sync_clock = false under [ups]. Setting the clock was confirmed on
    hardware by task T15.
  * argonctl rtc: read the UPS clock and its offset.

 -- Simon Keimer <dc0sk@mailbox.org>  Fri, 18 Sep 2026 14:20:00 +0200

argon-utils (0.1.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * argond can draw a status page on the case OLED: UPS charge and state,
    a charge bar, CPU temperature, fan, and the poweroff time when one is
    scheduled. Off by default; set [oled] enabled = true. A reading argond
    has stopped updating is shown as STALE, never as the last number.
  * argonctl oled --status previews that page in the terminal.

 -- Simon Keimer <dc0sk@mailbox.org>  Fri, 18 Sep 2026 12:30:00 +0200

argon-utils (0.1.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add argon-tray, a panel icon for the UPS state, CPU temperature and
    fan, started at login. Reads argond's status file; never opens the UPS
    port. Offers cancelling a scheduled poweroff, behind two clicks.
  * argonctl ups: explain the "Permission denied" when the packaged
    service owns the UPS port, and point at the status file instead.

 -- Simon Keimer <dc0sk@mailbox.org>  Fri, 18 Sep 2026 11:40:00 +0200

argon-utils (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial package: the argond system service, argonctl, the UPS udev
    rules and polkit rule, and the notification agent's autostart entry.
  * Ships mode = "read-only" deliberately: installing must not arm the
    low-battery poweroff. Set mode = "full" in /etc/argon-utils/config.toml.
  * Records and disables the vendor's UPS daemons, which cannot share the
    UPS serial port, and restores them on removal.

 -- Simon Keimer <dc0sk@mailbox.org>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 20:30:08 +0200
